From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 05:07:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Draw each structure as the thing it is, not as a row of boxes

The chassis owned seven panel constructors and six of them were a row of
boxes. There was nothing in it that could draw a parent-child edge, a
bucket with a floor, or a 2-D grid, so a tree problem could not come out
looking like a tree - the model picked the least-wrong thing available and
rendered a BST as chip rows grouped by depth. Two more things locked it in:
every folder was handed the same array problem as its worked example under
the instruction "match its shape exactly", and validate() rejected any panel
type it did not recognise. The animations were correct. The shapes were a lie.

Split the one question into three, so no step decides what it has no
business deciding:

  classify  reports WHICH STRUCTURES the code is made of, from a fixed enum
  visualize decides HOW TO DRAW THEM, freely, from the panel catalogue
  validate  checks only that nothing handed over went undrawn

lib/shapes.mjs holds all three halves of that table so they cannot drift
apart by being edited separately. Adding graph or DP panels later is an edit
to that one file plus a renderer in the chassis.

The chassis gains pTree, pList, pStack, pGrid and pHeap. All five read the
same CSS custom properties as the tile row, so palette and fonts are shared
by construction - the same argument that produced the chassis itself. Each
takes a `scale` from 0.7 to 1 so a wide structure fits one screen, clamped so
a panel can be made smaller but not unreadable. The chassis does every bit of
layout arithmetic; the generated definition supplies data only.

call-stack is a structure. Recursion is a mechanism rather than a data
structure, but a recursive descent is a stack, and a recursive solution drawn
without its frames is the most common way one of these teaches nothing.

Coverage enforcement is deliberately weak: any panel that can draw the
structure satisfies it, and only structures where a row of boxes is a real
loss are enforced. An array genuinely is a row of boxes. Where the code truly
never materialises a structure, a solution declares shapeOverride {skip,
reason}; a reason under 25 characters is refused, and every waiver is printed.

Also:
- the worked example is chosen for shape, so the first tree visualizer built
  becomes the example for every tree problem after it
- VISUALIZER_FORMAT, so the backfill knows what it has redrawn and terminates.
  "has a visualizer" was the whole test, which is why every array-shaped one
  counted as finished work
- select-backfill's copy of "is this classified" kept in step with classify's
- plain-text fields are checked for markup: &lt; written into a stack frame
  label arrived on screen character by character, and nothing caught it
- 46 new tests covering the coverage rule's edges

Verified end to end on lowest-common-ancestor-in-binary-search-tree: classify
gave optimal.cs -> tree and suboptimal.cs -> tree, call-stack off the code
alone, and visualize drew a real tree plus real recursion frames, waiving the
call stack on the iterative version with its own reasoning. Every panel type
was rendered in a browser; two layout faults and one crash found that way and
fixed.

